  "security_and_behavior_review": {
    "actual_defer_disposition": "A successful defer moves the task away from RUNNING to RETRY_WAIT, BLOCKED_EXTERNAL or OPERATIONAL_ERROR and clears owner and lease_until. It does not remain RUNNING.",
    "bounded_load_tradeoff": "A newly classified rollback may add at most two database sends within the existing policy. This is additional bounded database traffic and is not proof that monitor contention is repaired.",
    "existing_authorization_unchanged": "The database already authorizes backfill_defer for the worker. This change selects an existing transport rollback policy; it adds no operation grant, role, credential, trust relationship or endpoint.",
    "fences_and_budgets": "No task scope/cursor/page/generation/fence/attempt/reservation/deadline reset or renewal is introduced. Existing actor/capability checks run again for every sent request.",
    "managed_gateway_contract": "The actual PostgREST service was not exercised by local qualification, and effective external gateway environment is not observed. The prior explicit managed-gateway native-error contract assumption remains disclosed; it is not recast as measured configuration.",
    "not_idempotent": "No committed-defer acknowledgement branch exists. Once the disposition commits, the original owned-task check normally fails; safety depends on newly confirmed rollback rather than idempotence. A committed/lost ACK is still one send and fatal.",
    "provider_boundary": "The retry loop resends only the already serialized database RPC. It does not replay provider fetch, source acquisition or the enclosing worker unit.",
    "unchanged_transport": "The trusted HTTPS endpoint, nativeHTTP500/55P03 envelope/framing classifier, duplicate/nonfinite/truncation rejection, serialization, fixed0.25/0.75 delays, maximum3 sends, shared15-second deadline, response bounds, logs and ambiguous-result behavior are byte-identical to1deb.",
    "unknown_results": "Timeouts, lost acknowledgements, malformed/unknown responses, successful-response stream failures, wrong SQLSTATE/status/origin and controlled application errors cannot authorize a resend. Existing fatal behavior remains."
  },
